13:30:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The sensation of ears feeling "clogged" or popping is caused by a difference in air pressure between the middle ear and the environment. This pressure difference stretches the eardrum, and the body tries to equalize it by opening the Eustachian tubes (small passages connecting the middle ear to the back of the throat). When you travel by plane or drive up a mountain, the altitude changes quickly and significantly. For example: - A commercial airplane climbs hundreds of meters per minute, and the total pressure change from takeoff to cruising altitude can be more than 20% of atmospheric pressure. - Driving up a steep mountain road can involve a similar rapid altitude gain (e.g., 1,000 meters in 10–20 minutes), causing a noticeable pressure drop. These fast, large pressure changes can overwhelm the ability of the Eustachian tubes to equalize quickly enough, leading to the temporary clogged feeling. In contrast, an elevator in a tall building typically ascends at a slower rate (often around 2–10 meters per second) and covers a much smaller total height. Even the tallest buildings (e.g., Burj Khalifa at ~830 meters) represent only about a 10% pressure change from ground level, and the ride usually takes 1–2 minutes or more. This gradual, smaller pressure change allows your Eustachian tubes to equalize almost imperceptibly, so you rarely notice any clogging. Additionally, elevator shafts are not airtight—they have ventilation and gaps that allow air to move, so the pressure inside the elevator car closely matches the outside air at each floor. This further reduces any abrupt pressure difference. In contrast, airplane cabins are pressurized to a constant altitude (usually around 2,400 meters), so the pressure change during ascent and descent is more abrupt relative to the cabin’s internal pressure. In short: **The combination of slower rate, smaller total altitude change, and better equalization conditions in elevators prevents the noticeable ear-clogging effect that occurs in planes or vehicles climbing mountains.**
